    "title": "Berkeley Software Distribution",
    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution",
    "summary": "Unix operating system \"BSD\" redirects here. For other uses, see BSD (disambiguation) . Operating system BSD 4.3 BSD UNIX (from the University of Wisconsin ) man page of lisp. Developer CSRG Written in C OS family Unix Working state Discontinued Source model Open-source Initial release March 9, 1978 ; 48 years ago ( 1978-03-09 ) Final release 4.4-Lite2 / June 1995 ; 31 years ago ( 1995-06 ) Available in English Supported platforms PDP-11 , VAX , Intel 80386 Kernel type Monolithic Userland BSD Influenced 386BSD , NetBSD , FreeBSD , OpenBSD , NeXTSTEP , Darwin , Arch Linux Influenced by Unix Default user interface Unix shell License BSD licenses The Berkeley Software Distribution , also known as BSD or Berkeley UNIX , is a Unix based operating system developed and distributed by the Computer Systems Research Group at the University of California, Berkeley . BSD released in 1978, it began as an improved derivative of AT&T 's original Unix developed at Bell Labs . Bill Joy and Ozalp Babaoglu led early development. The BSD project received funding from DARPA until 1988. During this period, BSD incorporated ARPANET support and later implemented the TCP/IP protocol suite. ^ \"Why you should use a BSD style license for your Open Source Project\" . The FreeBSD Project . BSD (Berkeley Standard Distribution) . Retrieved August 3, 2021 . ^ Levitt, Jason; Schuman, Evan (June 22, 1992). \"For BSD Unix, It's Sayonara\" . Open Systems Today . ^ Harding, Elizabeth U. (October 19, 1992). \"Unix pioneer ends BSD research\" . Software Magazine . ^ a b Harding, Elizabeth U. (October 19, 1992). \"Unix pioneer ends BSD research\" . Software Magazine . ^ \"A Brief History of Unix, BSD and FreeBSD\" . minnie.tuhs.org . Retrieved December 5, 2024 . Cite error: There are <ref group=lower-alpha> tags or {{efn}} templates on this page, but the references will not show without a {{reflist|group=lower-alpha}} template or {{notelist}} template (see the help page ).",
    "text": "Unix operating system \"BSD\" redirects here. For other uses, see BSD (disambiguation) . Operating system BSD 4.3 BSD UNIX (from the University of Wisconsin ) man page of lisp. Developer CSRG Written in C OS family Unix Working state Discontinued Source model Open-source Initial release March 9, 1978 ; 48 years ago ( 1978-03-09 ) Final release 4.4-Lite2 / June 1995 ; 31 years ago ( 1995-06 ) Available in English Supported platforms PDP-11 , VAX , Intel 80386 Kernel type Monolithic Userland BSD Influenced 386BSD , NetBSD , FreeBSD , OpenBSD , NeXTSTEP , Darwin , Arch Linux Influenced by Unix Default user interface Unix shell License BSD licenses The Berkeley Software Distribution , also known as BSD or Berkeley UNIX , is a Unix based operating system developed and distributed by the Computer Systems Research Group at the University of California, Berkeley . BSD released in 1978, it began as an improved derivative of AT&T 's original Unix developed at Bell Labs . Bill Joy and Ozalp Babaoglu led early development. The BSD project received funding from DARPA until 1988. During this period, BSD incorporated ARPANET support and later implemented the TCP/IP protocol suite. ^ \"Why you should use a BSD style license for your Open Source Project\" . The FreeBSD Project . BSD (Berkeley Standard Distribution) . Retrieved August 3, 2021 . ^ Levitt, Jason; Schuman, Evan (June 22, 1992). \"For BSD Unix, It's Sayonara\" . Open Systems Today . ^ Harding, Elizabeth U. (October 19, 1992). \"Unix pioneer ends BSD research\" . Software Magazine . ^ a b Harding, Elizabeth U. (October 19, 1992). \"Unix pioneer ends BSD research\" . Software Magazine . ^ \"A Brief History of Unix, BSD and FreeBSD\" . minnie.tuhs.org . Retrieved December 5, 2024 . Cite error: There are <ref group=lower-alpha> tags or {{efn}} templates on this page, but the references will not show without a {{reflist|group=lower-alpha}} template or {{notelist}} template (see the help page ).",
